Date: Fri, 15 May 2026 01:23:03 -0500
Subject: Too many sites on Cloudflare

(not showing my email since urm... spam.
(infrequent, but still spam.))

NS endfindme.com now goes on Cloudflare too;
before it was Porkbun (though I *think* if
knowledge serves me right, Porkbun uses
Cloudflare in the background too.)

The reason: serve that rotten clubradar
project I've never had any interest in as
a PERMANENT redirect, without costing a
dime from my pocket!  I've heard of good
things about Cloudflare Worker from a guy
who's been running our university's last
and only web-development club (well, maybe a
bit excessive of an endorsement, considering
the whole CLUB's infrastructure runs on
Cloudflare: from registrar and DNS to website
and business logic code...), so I decided
to give it a shot.

(Funny sorry, following incidence of our
university's canvas.wisc.edu being caught
in the middle of the (at the very least
second) cyberattack against Instructure,
our DoIT imprudently used 301 instead of
302 or 307 to steer naive people away from
canvas.wisc.edu to alerts.wisc.edu.  Days
later (the following Tuesday, which is to
say the Tuesday this week) someone in the
UW-Madison Weixin group chat said their
browser just insisted on following that
redirect and won't go back to Canvas after
it got fixed.  Somebody suggested the guy
to ditch their browser session entirely
because DoIT essentially "poisoned" the
redirect cache. XD  So much for "protecting"
the students...)

So yes, the PERMANENT part is very much
intentional, in that I am "intentionally"
poisoning any of my pesky teammates who
cared enough to revisit for whatever reason.
Say that delivers a final goodbye kiss to
them for the unsightly irresponsibility.

But there is a catch with Cloudflare Worker
... to get the vanity domain working AND to
refrain from paying a dime, where money lacks
well your dignity makes up for.  Cloudflare
wants to manage my whole zone for me with
what's called their "Full DNS Setup". Without
it you either just can't use their service
with your domain (not even CNAME to the
worker.dev dynamic domain name!); or you'd
be paying them a fortune for a business plan
for a "Partial DNS Setup".  All for the basic
dignity to use a custom domain name.

..... *tsk* I sure do like it when people
frame it that way.  "Custom"... name.  I
mean it's your choice to make; sure.  You
own the network; I don't.  I just hate the
way it is phrased... as if we regular people
owning a little place on the Internet was the
exception rather than the norm.  Decentralized
communication *was* the norm, pumpkin head!
When the world was full of small sites and
providers respected our personal space like
Geocities... and you want to call FAANG and
mega-apps like Tencent Weixin the f-kn' norm.

Right now I've switched most of my *.net
domains away from Cloudflare; the ones that
I am serious about using internally at least.
I am going to try to find a balance with
things, but essentially... I don't want to
grow reliant on Cloudflare.  Not while I still
have to delete all my name records to upload
a new one from my locally-managed BIND zone
files (that is currently the only reason keeping
me from moving to something that feels more
"ethical", like deSEC.io); not while it randomly
rejects my OPENPGPKEY record for random errors
(and I mean random in a very literal sense --
you literally can't make out s**t from it);
and not while, unlike Netlify or Vercel, it
refuses to deploy my stuff to my domain because
it couldn't accept not having control over my
entire zone and have me jump through fancy
interfaces just to do the most simple basic
task; just minding my own business with my
DNS records.  Not while it asks to bind itself
to parts of my infrastructure more than what
I ask it to bind to.
